## Question 1

A Functional Interface is an interface that has exactly one abstract method. It can still have default methods, static methods, and methods inherited from `Object`.

The annotation used is `@FunctionalInterface`. It is not mandatory, but it is recommended because the compiler checks that the interface has only one abstract method.

Two built-in Functional Interfaces are:

- `Predicate<T>`: its abstract method is `boolean test(T t)`. It tests a value and returns `true` or `false`.
- `Function<T, R>`: its abstract method is `R apply(T t)`. It accepts a value of type `T` and returns a value of type `R`.

